    "title": "Payments are the bait, loans are the business",
    "lead": "Moneyview, a personal-loan app, listed this week at a 64% premium and is now worth about $1 billion. It isn't a one-off. India's free payments taught its fintechs to make their money from lending, and the stock market is now paying for that.",
    "body": "Start with something everyone knows: UPI is free. September saw 24 billion UPI payments, about 80 crore a day, and for most of them the shop pays nothing. That's great for shoppers, but it means moving money barely makes any money. So India's fintechs did the only thing that paid: they used payments to win users, then lent to them. Three forces now point the same way. First, lending is where the profit is. Moneyview made ₹242 crore profit on ₹3,351 crore revenue in FY26, and lent ₹23,099 crore (up 31%). Smaller lenders tell the same story: Seeds Fincap, which lends ₹50,000–₹1 lakh to people banks skip, has ₹722 crore in loans out, was profitable in FY25 and FY26, and just raised ₹100 crore. Second, the stock market only rewards profit. Big institutions bid 227× for Moneyview's shares, and loss-making AceVector got 3.4× from them. Third, payments may finally earn something. A 0.4% fee on shop payments above ₹2,000 (capped at ₹300) is due on 15 October. Traders called off their 'No UPI Day' protest only after meeting the Finance Minister, so the fee could still change. Here's the twist. The 'free UPI' that people say makes fintech unprofitable is actually what pushed India's fintechs into the profitable business of lending.",
    "why": "Razorpay, PhonePe and Fibe are all waiting to list. After Moneyview, investors will ask each one: how much of your money comes from loans, and are you profitable? What to watch: whether the 15 October fee starts on time, and whether it cuts UPI volume again in October. If it does, payment apps will lean even harder on lending. That means more credit for small borrowers, and more risk if those loans go bad."
